@Yourun-proger, this doesn't really answer his question:

> but I want to skip the heavy computations of the GET method as the body is not needed to answer quickly with just the headers


@erdnaxeli you can always check `request.method == 'HEAD'` in your code; I think that's actually better than separate functions because besides the lack of a body HEAD *should* be identical to GET.

I don't agree that this is an issue. `HEAD` is defined as the `GET` method without body. If you want to avoid a calculation as part of that, then you do if `request.method != "HEAD"` or similar. Werkzeug doesn't implement a separate HEAD function, it directly removes the body when generating the response.

It isn't clear how the result of a long computation would not contribute to the response headers for a GET request, since presumably you're doing that computation to GET results, which affects the content-length. If that's actually the case, it probably makes semantic sense to make that a POST request instead, since it's not a query at that point, it's kicking off a process, which is a side effect.

If you really don't want the GET route to handle the HEAD method, then you could remove the `HEAD` method from the rule after registering, or you could construct a `Rule` manually and add it.

```python
@app.route("/special")
def special():
    ...

for rule in app.url_map.iter_rules("special"):
    if rule.methods == {"GET", "HEAD", "OPTIONS"}:
        rule.methods.remove("HEAD")
```

```python
@app.endpoint("special")
def special():
    ...

rule = app.url_rule_class("/special", endpoint="special")
rule.methods = {"GET", "OPTIONS"}
rule.provide_automatic_options = True
app.url_map.add(rule)
```

