@allanlewis Note that the PyCharm issue you commented on where you said that the maintainers don't care to provide a fix for this was in fact opened by one of our core maintainers, and followed by another one (me). This is not an issue with Werkzeug, it's an issue with PyCharm, which has the huge complicated task of providing generic type and execution inference for all libraries in a dynamic language.

I usually don't use `abort` anyway, I just import the exception I want and raise it. pgjones offers a similar alternative.

@pgjones I don't think it's advisable to start implying raising `HTTPException` directly is good. This would also break Flask's error handler system, which relies on the type of the exception, not the code.

I don't plan to make any changes at this time, beyond adding type hinting. If existing hinting or PyCharm's system isn't working, that needs to be reported to typeshed or PyCharm.

